<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Khotan</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 1006 CE (approx.) · capital Khotan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The jade-rich Buddhist kingdom of the southern Silk Road, whose Saka-speaking kings lasted more than a thousand years until the Kara-Khanid conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Khotan, Yutian in Chinese, grew in the oasis watered by rivers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunlun-mountains">Kunlun Mountains</a> on the southern edge of the Taklamakan. Its own legends trace the founding to Indian and Chinese settlers, including a son of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. It appears in Han records from the 2nd century BCE, already trading the white and green jade of its rivers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Khotan's people spoke Khotanese, an Eastern Iranian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> language preserved in many Buddhist texts. Its kings, of the Visa dynasty, made it a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>, and tradition says a Chinese princess smuggled silkworm eggs to it in her headdress. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> it was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-garrisons-of-anxi">Four Garrisons of Anxi</a>; in the 10th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-shengtian">Li Shengtian</a> kept close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a long war, the Muslim <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a> conquered Khotan around 1006. Its Buddhist culture was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the Turkic language, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from Khotan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> preserve Khotanese literature.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-khotan.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Khotan" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Khotan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 1006 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khotan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Shengtian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khotanese Saka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khotan, Yutian</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=403&amp;focus=kingdom-of-khotan" aria-label="Kingdom of Khotan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6685 842 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Khotan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7005 1098L7771 1192M7005 1098L7372 1351M7005 1098L6820 915"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7771 1192h0M7372 1351h0M6820 915h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7005 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7005 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=403&amp;focus=kingdom-of-khotan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

